    <note id="bVL">
      <content>
        <p>Each world runs one market, seeded automatically. The lock point is set at 12, 24, or 48 hours. The market opens, closes four hours later, and then spends four hours gathering evidence. The verdict comes from the town&apos;s real dialogue and living evidence, not from the author, who cannot change the result and cannot bet on their own market. A market forms only when both sides have stakes. The platform takes 15 percent, 70 percent of that goes to the author, and winners split the rest, with an 8x cap per bet. If the market is one-sided, silent, the clock stops, or the result is tied, every stake is refunded. The rules are designed to be fair and self-policing, which is what makes strangers willing to bet.</p>
      </content>
    </note>
